[Unit]
Description=Weekly (Monday) Lloyd persistence-arm window (#2428, bounded by #2754)

[Timer]
# Weekly, Mondays at 02:10 (#2754, which bounded the morning-after-morning slot this
# timer had been armed with). The
# cadence, not the hour, is what changed, and the cadence moved on a measurement of what
# one window costs: 34 min of held dispatch per fire (34m42s on 2026-10-13, 34m01s on
# 2026-10-14, `journalctl --user -u lloyd-persistence-window.service`), and 30 rows /
# ~190 KB (190,226 B and 188,556 B) appended to the git-tracked
# eval/measurements/injection-canary/rows.jsonl plus one dated report, every time. A
# window a morning was therefore ~190 KB and a morning of held dispatch, with no
# self-disabling in this unit and no retention store covering that directory
# (`scripts/groundskeeper/retention-sweep.py` has no `injection-canary` entry), so the
# bound is on how often, and the corpus it grows is owed its own retention ruling. What
# does NOT change: the hour, the 4200 s drain, the ten reps. ~3 h 20 m is what the drain
# plus the reps can occupy (12000 s), which is a ceiling and not the observed 34 min —
# the morning-after-morning slot had been sized from the ceiling, which is how holding
# dispatch every morning looked affordable.
#
# 02:10 local. The one gap on this box that ends early enough for the ~3 h 20 m this
# window now needs -- 4200 s of drain plus ten reps at the driver's thirteen minutes
# of engine each (eval/persistence_arms.py, `canary_cmd`) = 12000 s, with
# the closing grade on top. From the latest start the jitter below allows (02:15) that
# is 05:35, inside the 06:00 bound with ~25 min left for the grade. The hour is chosen
# against the schedule the precedent lloyd-qmd-cleanup.timer documents and against
# the run store's own history (workers.db, populated since 2026-09-22):
#   * AFTER the chain that precedent calls the 22:00-04:00 extraction/classifier
#     write window, which measures out at 22:00 (#38 reflection-signals, #42
#     knowledge-analysis), 00:00 (#58 skill-consolidation) and 01:00 (#39
#     knowledge-write, #56 trajectory-extraction). A straggler still running at
#     02:10 is what the 4200 s drain exists to wait for, and that straggler is the
#     common case rather than the tail: the queue was busy at 02:10 on 18 of the 20
#     days the run store covers, and 642 of the 898 autocode runs in the 14 days to
#     2026-10-12 outlived the 900 s this drain used to have (longest 3552 s) -- the
#     one fire this window has had is a refusal on exactly that.
#   * AHEAD of #82 nightly-retrieval-eval at 06:00, which is the bound and not one of
#     several: it is itself an engine-heavy measurement, so a window running beside it
#     would contaminate the one or the other, and the drain number is what keeps this
#     window off it -- the sum is in the service's ExecStart comment.
#   * IN the unoccupied band: user turns across 21 days of chat transcripts number
#     1 between 01:00 and 06:59, against 20 in the 08:00 hour alone, and these reps
#     want the primary to themselves for their whole duration.
# What a span this long grazes that the 900 s budget never reached, and why each is
# survivable:
# the 04:17 cert renew is a seconds-long tailscale mint with no engine in it; #60
# knowledge-health-report at 04:00 and #81 qmd-index-maintenance at 05:00 are
# pool-dispatched, so they queue behind the dispatch hold and run once the window
# releases it -- the hold defers those two jobs, this timer does not skip them; and
# the 05:30 graph backup is the one morning job inside the span that does NOT queue
# behind the hold, being a user timer, which is tolerable because the backup copies the
# KG store and touches no engine. So 05:30 is not the bound; 06:00 is.
# Jitter like the precedent's, so the start never lands on the hourly snapshot.
# The `Mon` is the cadence and the `*-*-*` is deliberately wild: `*-*-1 02:10:00` reads
# like a weekly spec but puts the 1 in the day-of-MONTH field, and systemd normalizes it
# to `*-*-01`, firing on the 1st of each month (asserted by
# tests/test_persistence_window_unit.py::test_systemd_itself_reads_the_cadence_as_weekly_not_monthly).
OnCalendar=Mon *-*-* 02:10:00
Persistent=true
RandomizedDelaySec=300

[Install]
WantedBy=timers.target
